 ### Beveled Chunk Meshing
-A meshing technique where every block is drawn as a beveled cube with its own panel, a dark groove where it meets neighbors, and ambient occlusion in the corners, forming a closed surface without greedy merging [S3]. For each block, it generates up to four kinds of pieces: a panel per exposed face, a strip per exposed edge, a triangle per exposed corner, and a small cap where four blocks meet along an edge [S3]. Faces and chamfers hidden by neighbors are dropped to ensure a wall is one closed surface with a groove between blocks [S3]. Caps are required to close the diamond shaft left by four chamfered blocks around an edge [S3]. The hot path must allocate nothing to maintain performance [S3]. A typical chunk takes 0.3 to 1.3 ms to mesh; a very dense chunk (hollow room of 28k triangles) takes about 7 ms [S3]. Choose it for stylized voxel rendering that requires closed manifolds and per-block grooves without greedy merging.
